# What gets uploaded to Cloud Build by `gcloud run deploy --source .`.
#
# This file exists mostly to stop a surprise: with no .gcloudignore, gcloud
# silently DERIVES one from .gitignore. That happens to be safe here — .gitignore
# excludes .env — but "safe by coincidence" is not how secrets should work, and
# the derived behaviour is invisible to whoever reads this repo next.
#
# Two separate layers, both needed:
#   .gcloudignore  — what leaves this machine for Cloud Build
#   .dockerignore  — what the Docker build inside Cloud Build can see

# SECRETS. ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY. Secret Manager, not a layer.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# The 100 MB database is regenerated inside the image, so it stays out. But
# `data/cache/` MUST go in: it holds the three Overpass/OpenStreetMap responses
# (748 KB) that the datagen builds the road network from, and `osm.py` never
# re-fetches a cached file.
#
# Excluding all of `data/` is what broke the first Cloud Build — the datagen
# fell through to a live Overpass call and got `0 elements` back from a flaky
# mirror. Shipping the cache also pins the deployed world to the same road
# network the demo was developed against, instead of whatever Overpass happens
# to return on build day.
data/*
!data/cache

# Rebuilt in the image's `web` stage.
web/dist/
web/node_modules/

var/
*.log

.git/
.venv/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.DS_Store

# Dev-only. pytest is in the `dev` dependency group and the image runs
# `uv sync --no-dev`, so these could not run there anyway.
tests/
